{%- comment -%}
  Finds the current page's twin in the other language and sets two variables:

    twin_url     the mirrored address, e.g. /cv/ <-> /it/cv/
    twin_exists  whether a page is actually built there

  It lives in its own file because two places need the same answer -- the
  language switch in header.liquid and the hreflang links in head.liquid -- and
  a second copy of this rule would be free to drift from the first. That is not
  hypothetical: the navbar used to pick the right page and then send the reader
  to a hardcoded '/blog/', because the address was decided twice.

  Both sides are normalised before comparing: jekyll-paginate-v2 reports URLs
  that end in index.html, so /it/blog/index.html has to match /it/blog/.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign here = page.url | remove: 'index.html' -%}
{%- if page.lang == 'it' -%}
  {%- assign twin_url = here | remove_first: '/it' -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign twin_url = '/it' | append: here -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- assign twin_exists = false -%}
{%- assign everything = site.documents | concat: site.pages -%}
{%- for doc in everything -%}
  {%- assign doc_url = doc.url | remove: 'index.html' -%}
  {%- if doc_url == twin_url -%}
    {%- assign twin_exists = true -%}
    {%- break -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
